\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Depth Classification Performance}
\label{sec:results-classification}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves 3-class LOGO F1\,=\,0.762 (95\% CI [0.758, 0.766]; $N$\,=\,37{,}058) across eight generators from five families (Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}).
Because gen-0 seeds are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}), gen-0 classification is near-perfect (per-class F1\,$\approx$\,0.94) and the 3-class metric is inflated by this overlap.
We therefore adopt leak-free binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+)\,=\,0.694, CI [0.688, 0.700], as the primary metric throughout; it excludes gen-0 entirely and is unaffected by seed sharing.
Per-generator binary F1 ranges from 0.633 (Mistral-7B) to 0.724 (Pythia-2.8B); the primary difficulty is distinguishing gen-1 from gen-2+ (Appendix~\ref{app:confusion}).
Larger generators show moderate degradation (Qwen3-8B: 0.692, Mistral-7B: 0.633) relative to the 0.694 average.
The Pythia-1B/2.8B crossover between three-class and binary scores (0.806 vs.\ 0.798 and 0.718 vs.\ 0.724, respectively) reflects differences in gen-0 classification accuracy rather than in depth discrimination per se.

\paragraph{Cross-model generalization.}
Cross-model chains (randomly selected generator per step) yield 3-class F1\,=\,0.809 and binary F1\,=\,0.764 under 5-fold CV.\footnote{Cross-model chains use the original five-generator subset (Pythia, OLMo, Qwen); Llama and Mistral were added later for single-generator evaluation.}
This protocol differs from LOGO (all generators appear in training), so the difference from single-generator LOGO (binary 0.694) reflects both protocol and generator-subset effects; the 5-generator LOGO average is 0.734 for reference.
Under class balancing, macro F1 reaches 0.831 (per-class breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-detail}).

Leave-one-family-out (LOFO) evaluation yields average binary F1\,=\,0.686, which confirms transfer across architectural families.
A scale effect emerges: Pythia generators achieve the highest binary F1 (0.718--0.724), while larger models show degradation (Mistral-7B: 0.633), consistent with higher-capacity models compressing the feature margins DDR exploits.
The signal generalizes across text domains with variation (Appendix~\ref{app:domain}).

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Length-matched subsampling yields binary F1\,=\,0.724 (vs.\ 0.770 unmatched); a random-subsample control confirms the drop reflects deconfounding, not sample-size reduction (Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

\paragraph{Seed-sharing control.}
To verify that depth classification does not rely on seed-specific topic information, we evaluate two controls.
First, seed-level demeaning (subtracting each seed's gen-0 feature values as a per-seed baseline) yields binary F1\,=\,0.701, indistinguishable from the undemeaned baseline (0.694 under the same evaluation protocol).
Second, an independent-seed experiment using completely disjoint seed pools per generator yields binary F1\,=\,0.680 (Pythia${\rightarrow}$OLMo: 0.695, OLMo${\rightarrow}$Pythia: 0.666; Appendix~\ref{app:seed-control}), a modest reduction consistent with cross-generator transfer cost rather than topic leakage.
Both controls confirm that the classifier captures generative degradation patterns rather than seed-specific topic content.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Family} & \textbf{Params} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
Pythia-2.8B     & Pythia  & 2.8B & 0.798 & \textbf{0.724} & [.707, .742] \\
Pythia-1B       & Pythia  & 1.0B & \textbf{0.806} & 0.718 & [.700, .735] \\
Qwen3-1.7B     & Qwen    & 1.7B & 0.772 & 0.709 & [.693, .726] \\
Llama-3.2-3B   & Llama   & 3.2B & 0.775 & 0.697 & [.680, .713] \\
OLMo-2-1B      & OLMo    & 1.5B & 0.781 & 0.695 & [.679, .711] \\
Qwen3-8B       & Qwen    & 8.2B & 0.714 & 0.692 & [.674, .708] \\
Qwen3-0.6B     & Qwen    & 0.6B & 0.780 & 0.691 & [.673, .709] \\
Mistral-7B     & Mistral & 7.0B & 0.682 & 0.633 & [.617, .648] \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Average (8-gen LOGO)}} & \textbf{0.762} & \textbf{0.694} & [.688, .700] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{8-generator LOGO macro-F1 for the 7-feature ensemble (bootstrap 95\% CIs, $n$\,=\,1000). Binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) is the primary metric. Note: 3-class F1 may carry upward bias from gen-0 seed sharing across generators; binary F1 is leak-free. Cross-model chains (Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref}) use sample-level CV without this issue.}
\label{tab:logo-results}
\end{table}

\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

Features exhibit \emph{differential depth response} (DDR): central-tendency and diversity measures drift monotonically with depth, while shape measures remain near-stationary, consistent with recursive generation inducing a location shift in the NLL distribution with distributional shape remaining comparatively stable (shape-feature CV shift $<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\% for drifting features).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response: Spearman correlations between individual features and generation depth. Central-tendency and diversity features (hapax ratio, TTR, entropy mean) show substantial monotonic drift; entropy std ($|\rho|{=}0.17$) occupies an intermediate position; shape features (entropy max, tail decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled class-balanced Spearman correlations with depth confirm the split (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}):
drifting features show substantial monotonic trends (hapax ratio $\rho = -0.63$, TTR $\rho = -0.61$, entropy mean $\rho = -0.60$),
while shape features remain near-stationary (tail decay $|\rho| = 0.04$).
The six-fold magnitude gap between drifting ($|\rho| > 0.60$) and shape features ($|\rho| < 0.10$) serves as the operative criterion for the DDR split, with entropy std occupying an intermediate position ($|\rho| = 0.17$); shifting these descriptive thresholds by $\pm 0.10$ does not alter feature grouping.

\paragraph{Distributional shift quantification.}
Two-sample KS tests between gen-1 and gen-2+ distributions confirm the split: drifting-feature KS statistics (0.516--0.537) are ${\sim}10\times$ larger than shape-feature statistics (0.043--0.052; all $p \approx 0$ after Bonferroni correction).
Levene tests reject equal variance for all features ($p < 10^{-10}$), but effect sizes differ markedly: shape-feature CV shift $<$3\% vs.\ $>$15\% for drifting features, which supports ``comparatively stable'' as a practical characterization rather than a strict invariance claim.

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
Under 10-fold stratified CV, the full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773) outperforms surface-only (F1\,=\,0.688) and reference-only (F1\,=\,0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Table~\ref{tab:ablation} in Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table}).
Reference-only yields higher binary F1 (0.833 vs.\ 0.695) since surface features introduce noise at the gen-1/gen-2+ boundary, but surface features are essential for 3-class gen-0 discrimination (Full 0.773 vs.\ Reference-only 0.713).
Permutation importance: hapax ratio (0.35), entropy mean (0.23), TTR (0.18); leave-one-feature-out confirms 6 of 7 features contribute positively (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).
TTR and hapax ratio are strongly correlated ($r = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}), reducing the effective feature dimensionality to approximately five; both are retained because LOFO analysis shows each contributes marginal but positive signal ($-0.55$ and $-0.42$\,pp respectively; Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) achieve comparable LOGO F1 (spread 0.013; Table~\ref{tab:cross-ref} in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}), with consistently negative entropy drift; this confirms DDR is a property of recursive generation.

\paragraph{Temperature sensitivity.}
Performance varies with temperature in a model-dependent manner (Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}): Pythia-2.8B improves at $t{=}0.5$ (LOGO binary F1\,=\,0.814 vs.\ 0.740 at $t{=}0.7$), while Qwen3-1.7B shows negligible change (0.751 at both temperatures).
Both degrade sharply at $t{=}0.9$ (Pythia: $-$26.0\,pp; Qwen: $-$18.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}), and entropy mean's Spearman correlation with depth weakens from $\rho = -0.60$ to $\rho = -0.30$, consistent with higher-temperature sampling injecting randomness that masks the location shift DDR exploits.

\paragraph{Prompt-type sensitivity.}
The depth signal is prompt-type dependent: continuation chains show the strongest drift (within-type binary F1 = 0.781), followed by elaboration (0.730) and rewriting (0.737). Paraphrastic chains yield weaker signal (binary F1 = 0.671 vs.\ 0.749 for continuation), approaching the method's detection floor. Cross-prompt-type transfer (LOPTO F1 = 0.753) confirms shared signal across prompt types (Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}).


\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

To contextualize the 7-feature ensemble's discriminative power, we repurpose four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors using the same XGBoost framework and LOGO splits (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 with 95\% bootstrap CIs. Per-generator variance is higher for single detectors than for the ensemble.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation: predictions from all held-out folds are concatenated before computing F1, which yields slightly higher scores than the per-generator macro average in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}). All methods use identical XGBoost configuration ($n_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max_\text{depth}{=}6$). Binary: gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+. Inference cost: 7-feature requires one 410M reference model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles require two models totaling 1.4B parameters (2{,}868\,MB).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble exceeds all individual baselines in 3-class F1 ($\Delta$\,=\,6.7--14.4\,pp vs.\ Avg-LogProb through LD-Score).
Multi-detector ensembles achieve higher 3-class F1 (0.795 vs.\ 0.774, +2.1\,pp) and marginally higher binary F1 (0.708 vs.\ 0.705, +0.3\,pp), but require 2.4$\times$ longer inference and 3.2$\times$ more GPU memory.


Preliminary downstream evaluation (fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on depth-stratified data; Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}) finds that depth composition does not determine training utility at fixed sample count, though the evidence is limited (three seeds, single perplexity metric).

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig2_framework.pdf}
\caption{Depth fingerprinting framework. (a)~Recursive generation chains are constructed by iteratively feeding text through a generator $G$. (b)~Seven features are extracted per text: five NLL statistics from a Pythia-410M reference model and two surface diversity measures. Horizontal bars show Spearman $\rho$ with generation depth and reveal differential depth response: drifting features ($|\rho| > 0.6$) capture the monotonic location shift, while stationary features ($|\rho| < 0.1$) provide a stable baseline. (c)~An XGBoost classifier trained under Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO) cross-validation predicts depth class (gen-0\,/\,gen-1\,/\,gen-2+).}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task Formulation}
\label{sec:task}

Given a text $x$, the goal is to predict its \emph{generation depth} $d \in \{0, 1, 2{+}\}$, where $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text, $d{=}1$ denotes first-generation LLM output conditioned on human text, and $d{=}2{+}$ denotes text produced by recursively conditioning an LLM on its own prior output at least twice.
Formally, let $\mathcal{C}_0$ be a reference corpus of human-written text.
A generation chain of depth $k$ is defined recursively: $x^{(0)} \sim \mathcal{C}_0$, and $x^{(k)} = G(x^{(k-1)})$ for $k \geq 1$, where $G$ denotes stochastic LLM generation conditioned on its input.
We frame depth estimation as an ordinal classification task over three classes rather than binary real-vs-synthetic detection, which enables finer-grained provenance characterization.
Depths beyond 2 are merged into a single $2{+}$ class, consistent with monotonic feature convergence that compresses inter-class margins beyond depth 2 (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}).

\subsection{Generation Chain Construction}
\label{sec:generation}

We construct generation chains using eight generators spanning five model families: \textbf{Pythia} (2.8B, 1B) \cite{pythia}, \textbf{OLMo} (OLMo-2-1B) \cite{olmo}, \textbf{Qwen} (Qwen3-1.7B, Qwen3-0.6B, Qwen3-8B-Base) \cite{qwen3}, \textbf{Llama} (Llama-3.2-3B) \cite{llama3}, and \textbf{Mistral} (Mistral-7B-v0.3) \cite{mistral7b}.
This selection covers parameter scales from 0.6B to 8B and diverse pretraining corpora, which enables evaluation of cross-generator and cross-scale generalization.

For each generator, we sample approximately 1{,}000 seed passages from English Wikipedia as $\mathcal{C}_0$.
The same pool of seed passages serves as gen-0 for all generators.
We use shared seeds across generators to ensure that cross-generator comparisons control for input content, which isolates the effect of the generation model from source text variation.
In LOGO evaluation, gen-0 samples from the held-out generator's chains also appear (with the same text) in the training generators' chains, creating a potential train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We address this with a supplementary binary evaluation excluding gen-0 entirely (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Each seed is iteratively fed through the generator to produce a chain of depth 4 (i.e., gen-0 through gen-4), using the prompt ``\texttt{Continue the following text: \{text\}}''.
All generation uses temperature $\tau{=}0.7$, nucleus sampling with $p{=}0.9$, and maximum length of 256 new tokens.
The theoretical yield is $8 \times {\sim}1{,}000 \times 5 = 39{,}815$ texts; after removing 2{,}746 chain breaks (empty or degenerate outputs at intermediate depths that prevented deeper generation) and 11 passages too short for feature extraction, 37{,}058 texts remain (Appendix~\ref{app:dataset-stats}).
For classification, depths 2--4 are collapsed into the $2{+}$ class.

\subsection{Feature Extraction}
\label{sec:features}

The seven features are motivated by two complementary views of distributional drift.
Reference-model features capture how the text's token-level surprise distribution shifts relative to a fixed model; surface features capture how lexical diversity contracts under recursive generation.
Together, they span both the generative mechanism (what the model produces) and the surface manifestation (how the vocabulary changes); Figure~\ref{fig:framework} provides an overview.
This two-pronged design is validated through leave-one-feature-out analysis (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}) and cross-group ablation (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}), which confirms that both feature groups contribute to depth discrimination.

We extract seven distributional features per text, organized into two groups based on what they measure rather than how they are computed.

\paragraph{Reference-model features.}
A held-out reference model (Pythia-410M, chosen to be smaller than all generators) computes per-token negative log-likelihood (NLL) for each text.
We use ``entropy'' as shorthand for token-level NLL throughout, following convention in prior detection work; these are point-wise NLL values, not Shannon entropy of the full distribution.
Although Pythia-410M shares the GPT-NeoX architecture with two of the eight generators, cross-family reference model experiments (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) confirm that depth features are not reference-specific (LOGO F1 varies by $\leq$0.013 across Pythia, OLMo, and Qwen references).
From the resulting NLL sequence, we extract five statistics:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{entropy\_mean}: mean token-level NLL, which captures the average predictability of the text under the reference model;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_std}: standard deviation of token-level NLL, which measures the spread of per-token surprise;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_max}: maximum token-level NLL, which captures the most surprising token;
    \item \textbf{entropy\_min}: minimum token-level NLL, which captures the most predictable token. As generation depth increases, the floor of token-level surprise drops; this reflects the model's increasing confidence on its own outputs.
    Retained because removing it does not improve performance (LOFO $\Delta$F1\,=\,+0.01\,pp, within bootstrap noise) and it completes the four-number NLL summary (mean, std, max, min), which ensures the classifier has access to the full marginal distribution shape;
    \item \textbf{tail\_decay}: slope of the vocabulary-level averaged log-probability distribution in the mid-rank region (ranks 100--1000).
    For each text, we compute the mean log-probability of each vocabulary token across all positions under the reference model, sort tokens in descending order of mean log-probability, and fit a linear regression to ranks 100--1000.
    The slope captures how steeply the reference model's probability mass decays beyond high-probability tokens and characterizes the shape of the distribution tail.
    We select ranks 100--1000 because high-frequency tokens (ranks $<$ 100) exhibit minimal discriminative variance across depths, while ranks beyond 1000 are sparsely populated and noisy.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Surface features.}
Two features capture lexical diversity without requiring a reference model:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{TTR}: type-token ratio (number of unique word types divided by total tokens);
    \item \textbf{hapax\_ratio}: proportion of words appearing exactly once (\emph{hapax legomena}).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The grouping reflects an empirical pattern we term \emph{differential depth response}: central-tendency and diversity features (entropy\_mean, TTR, hapax\_ratio) show substantial monotonic drift with generation depth ($|\rho| = 0.60$--$0.63$); entropy\_min and entropy\_std show moderate drift ($|\rho| = 0.17$--$0.41$); and shape features (entropy\_max, tail\_decay) remain near-stationary ($|\rho| < 0.10$).
This differential behavior, consistent with recursive generation shifting the location of the NLL distribution with distributional shape remaining comparatively stable, motivates combining both feature types (pairwise feature correlations in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}).
As shown in \S\ref{sec:results}, the full 7-feature set significantly outperforms either group alone.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\paragraph{Cross-validation.}
To evaluate generalization to unseen generators, we use \textbf{Leave-One-Generator-Out (LOGO)} cross-validation over all eight generators; \textbf{Leave-One-Family-Out (LOFO)} holds out entire model families.
Cross-model chains use the original five-generator subset (Pythia-2.8B/1B, OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-1.7B/0.6B); Llama and Mistral were added subsequently for single-generator LOGO only.
Some appendix analyses predate this expansion and use six generators (the original five plus Qwen3-8B); each table notes its generator subset.

\paragraph{Classifier.}
We use XGBoost \cite{xgboost} throughout ($n\_\text{estimators}{=}200$, $\max\_\text{depth}{=}6$, learning rate $0.1$), with identical hyperparameters across all methods in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}.
LOGO F1 is stable across random seeds (std\,$<$\,0.001 over 5 seeds).
Alternative classifiers and ordinal-specific methods (CORAL \cite{coral_ordinal}, CORN \cite{corn_ordinal}, Frank-Hall \cite{frank_hall_ordinal}) yield comparable performance (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}), which confirms that discriminative power resides in the features.

\paragraph{Metrics and statistical testing.}
The primary metric is \textbf{macro-averaged F1} over the three depth classes.
We report 95\% confidence intervals via percentile bootstrap ($n{=}1{,}000$), McNemar tests for pairwise comparisons, and paired bootstrap for baseline comparisons.
All $p$-values are two-sided; Bonferroni correction is applied within each family of simultaneous tests: the 21 distributional comparisons (7 features $\times$ 3 depth-group pairs, \S\ref{sec:results-differential}) and the 4 pairwise baseline comparisons (\S\ref{sec:results-baselines}).
Feature--depth correlations (\S\ref{sec:results-differential}) use class-balanced subsamples to control for depth-class imbalance.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare the 7-feature ensemble against four single-score detection methods repurposed as depth predictors: Avg-LogProb, FastDetectGPT \cite{fast_detectgpt}, Binoculars \cite{binoculars}, and LD-Score \cite{ld_score}.
Each baseline score is used as a univariate feature in the same XGBoost framework under LOGO cross-validation to ensure a controlled comparison.

\paragraph{Length confound control.}
Since TTR and hapax\_ratio are mechanically length-dependent, we verify depth signal is not a length artifact via length-matched subsampling that equalizes length distributions across depth classes (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}; Appendix~\ref{app:length}).

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced generation depth classification and documented a differential depth response across eight base-model generators from five families: recursive generation is consistent with a systematic location shift in the token-level NLL distribution whose shape remains comparatively stable.
A lightweight 7-feature ensemble detects this drift with leak-free binary F1\,=\,0.694 under eight-generator LOGO evaluation (0.764 on cross-model chains; five-generator subset).
The signal degrades substantially under instruction tuning and may further compress at larger scales (our largest generator is 8B); these limitations define the current scope boundaries.
All code, data, and trained models will be released upon publication.

\section*{Ethics Statement}
\label{sec:ethics}

All texts analyzed in this work are LLM-generated from publicly available Wikipedia seeds; no personal or sensitive data is involved.
Our method estimates the generation depth of a text passage (how many recursive generation steps it has undergone), not the identity of its author.
Individual authorship attribution is outside both the scope and the capability of this approach; privacy implications are minimal.
We acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be used to selectively suppress machine-generated text in contexts where such content is legitimate (e.g., accessibility tools, assistive writing).
We encourage deployment in data governance pipelines where provenance transparency, rather than content removal, is the objective.

\paragraph{Licenses.}
All generator models are publicly available under permissive licenses (Apache 2.0 for Pythia, OLMo, and Mistral; Qwen License for Qwen3; Llama Community License for Llama 3.2).
Wikipedia seed passages are distributed under CC BY-SA 3.0.

\paragraph{Compute budget.}
All experiments were conducted on 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32\,GB each), totaling approximately 62 GPU-hours.

\paragraph{Software.}
Experiments used Python 3.12.3, PyTorch 2.10.0 (CUDA 12.8), Transformers 4.57.6, XGBoost 3.2.0, scikit-learn 1.8.0, NumPy 2.2.6, pandas 3.0.2, and SciPy 1.17.1.

